      id: "ch11-t3",
      sectionNumber: "11.3",
      sectionTitle: "General Juristic Rules of Ijarah",
      title: "General Juristic Rules of Ijarah: Valid Subject Matter and Conditions",
      overview: "Details which assets can be leased (not consumed with use), why money/edibles/fuel cannot be leased, and Al-Kasani's list of essential validity conditions.",
      simpleExplanation: "You can only lease things that give benefit without being 'used up' - like a house or a car, not money or food, because using those things means consuming them entirely (which makes the transaction effectively a loan, and any extra charged on top would be interest). For a lease to be valid: both parties must agree, the asset must be clearly known, the lease period must be specified, using the asset must actually be possible, the asset must be handed over before rent is owed, and the purpose must be lawful.",
      academicExplanation: "Jurists are nearly unanimous that Ijarah is valid only for things possessing Manafa'ah (usufruct/benefit) whose corpus/substance ('Ayn) is not consumed by use - goods like candles, cotton, food, fuel are suitable for SALE, not leasing. Kasani explains dirhams, dinars, bullion (which are 'Ain, not usufruct) and anything whose benefit requires consumption cannot be leased; further, the genus of the leased asset and the rent should not be the same (e.g. house-for-house, ride-for-ride). If money/edibles/fuel/ammunition are 'leased,' the transaction is deemed a loan, and any rent charged on it amounts to interest. Assets from which usufruct is virtually impossible (e.g. land too saline to produce) also cannot be leased. Validity requires mutual consent, the asset belonging to the lessor (as owner or as a permitted sub-lessor), freedom from Gharar regarding the nature of usufruct/consideration, and mutual knowledge of the contracted usufruct's nature. Rent can only be fixed once the property is known (via inspection, viewing, or description); it's permissible to stipulate expedited or deferred/delayed rent payment terms as agreed. Al-Kasani's key validity conditions: the contracted usufruct must be ascertained to avoid dispute; the lease period must be specified (for wage/service work, either the amount of work or the time period must be known); benefiting from the hired goods must actually be possible (leasing a nonexistent asset whose usufruct can't be precisely described is disallowed, due to Gharar); handing over/delivery of the leased goods is essential - NO rent becomes due merely upon contract execution unless the subject is delivered and made available (though advance rent CAN be taken once availability for the lease period is ensured); the hired party (for services) must be capable of the job (hiring a runaway animal or usurped assets is invalid); the usufruct must be lawful (not Haram purpose); the usufruct should follow local convention/tradition. On WHEN rent becomes due: Hanafi jurists hold rent is due only once usufruct actually emerges (i.e. after delivery in usable form), while Shafi'i jurists hold rent becomes due upon contract finalization (though they too presume usufruct materializes through the lessee's use) - in practice, even under the Shafi'i view rent becomes due once the asset is possessed and usable by the lessee. If the asset to be leased is yet to be purchased at the prospective lessee's request, the lessor can demand earnest money to secure the lessee's commitment; if the lessee breaches this promise, the lessor may retain the amount of loss actually incurred (processing costs, purchase costs, cost of disposing/re-leasing elsewhere), returning any excess. Per AAOIFI, rental can be paid in instalments or as mutually agreed, and advance rent (effectively serving as earnest money at contract execution) is permissible.",
      examExplanation: "Ijarah valid ONLY for assets whose usufruct doesn't consume the corpus (not money, edibles, fuel, ammunition - leasing these = deemed a loan, rent on them = Riba). Al-Kasani's conditions: ascertained usufruct, specified lease period, usufruct must actually be achievable (no Gharar), delivery required before rent accrues (though advance rent is allowed once availability is ensured), capable subject (no runaway animals/usurped assets), lawful purpose, conventional usufruct. Rent timing debate: Hanafi = due only after usufruct emerges (post-delivery, usable form); Shafi'i = due at contract finalization (but converges with Hanafi in practice, since usufruct is presumed to materialize via use).",
