      title: "Execution of an Ijarah Contract: Instant vs Future Enforcement",
      overview: "Explains when an Ijarah contract can be signed for future enforcement (unlike Bai'), and why a specific but not-yet-owned asset requires special treatment via a promise to lease.",
      simpleExplanation: "Unlike a sale (which must happen immediately), a lease can be agreed now but take effect later, because ownership stays with the lessor the whole time anyway - the risk of the asset is still theirs. But there's a catch: if the lease is for one SPECIFIC asset that the bank doesn't yet own, they can't sign the actual lease contract until they've actually acquired it. In the meantime, they can only make a 'promise to lease,' and any advance rent taken isn't counted as real rent yet - it just gets applied later once rent actually starts.",
      academicExplanation: "Depending on the asset's nature, an Ijarah contract can be executed before or after the lessor takes possession, for instant or future enforcement/commencement. If the asset already exists (e.g. an existing liveable house), the lease contract can be for instant or future enforcement, since its usufruct is clear to both parties who can stipulate rental accordingly. Future enforcement is allowed in Ijarah (unlike Bai') because ownership remains with the lessor throughout, who bears damage risk. Similarly, in Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (where the asset's nature/quality is specified rather than a particular unit, and destruction of one unit doesn't terminate the contract), the contract can be for instant or future enforcement. HOWEVER, if a PARTICULAR asset is specified for Ijarah, the lease contract cannot be executed before the lessor obtains ownership/possession of that asset (or its usufruct, in sub-lease cases) - if the asset is destroyed before this, the contract terminates. If the lessor does not yet own the asset, he can instead enter a PROMISE to lease with the prospective lessee; advance rent taken at this stage is NOT considered accrued rent - it is simply adjusted against rental becoming due later. Islamic banks' leases normally fall into this category. Per the promise, the lessor can purchase the asset from the market or, in special cases, from the promisee himself - in which case it becomes a 'sale and lease-back' arrangement, and the sale/purchase contract must precede the lease contract (the Ijarah contract must not be stipulated as a precondition of the sale contract).",
