      simpleExplanation: "Banks are allowed to set rent based on what they paid for the asset, as long as both sides agree - and once fixed, the bank can't just raise the rent on its own. For long leases, rent can even be tied to something that changes over time, like inflation or a market benchmark - and surprisingly, the book argues that using an interest-rate benchmark like LIBOR just to set the NUMBER doesn't make the lease un-Islamic, as long as the lessor still genuinely bears the ownership risk (the real difference between a real lease and a disguised loan). To avoid disputes, any variable-rent formula should have a cap and floor so rent can't swing wildly.",
      academicExplanation: "Determining rental based on the lessor's aggregate cost of purchase/construction/installation is not against Shari'ah if both parties agree and all other valid-lease conditions are met. Once agreed, the lessor cannot unilaterally increase rent (any contrary agreement is void), though different rates for different phases can be agreed via a benchmark, and both parties can mutually agree during the lease to review the period or rental. In long-duration leases, scholars allow rent increases after specified periods, tying rent to a variable well-defined reference rate/benchmark, or periodic percentage increases (e.g. 5% yearly) - a well-defined benchmark/price index is recommended for subsequent periods to avoid dispute/injustice from market fluctuations, given the lease's binding nature. Rent can also be tied to tax increases or inflation rates. On the LIBOR question: a minority of scholars object that using an interest-rate benchmark makes the transaction resemble interest and is therefore impermissible - the author explicitly rejects this view, arguing that as long as basic Shari'ah requirements are met, ANY benchmark can be used to price sale or lease transactions; benchmarking to an interest-based rate does not itself render the transaction Haram. The rationale: the essential difference between valid lease and interest-based financing is that the LESSOR assumes full risk of the leased asset's corpus - if the asset loses usufruct without lessee misuse/negligence, the lessor cannot claim rent and bears the loss (whereas in interest-based lease financing, the lessee bears all ownership-related expense/responsibility). As long as this risk-bearing distinction is maintained, using an interest-rate index as a mere pricing benchmark does not recategorize the transaction as interest-bearing - though it is DESIRABLE to prefer non-interest benchmarks so an Islamic transaction is visibly distinguished. A floating rental structure is valid provided the FIRST period's rental amount is specified. To avoid Gharar/Jahala, scholars recommend a ceiling/floor limiting how much the rent-benchmark relationship can swing (e.g. rent cannot increase or decrease by more than 5%).",
