      title: "Sub-leasing, Security, and Liability Allocation Between Lessor and Lessee",
      overview: "Covers the permissibility of sub-leasing (and the scholarly dispute over keeping a sub-lease profit margin), the lessor's right to demand security/guarantees, and the clear-cut liability split between ownership expenses (lessor) and usage expenses (lessee).",
      simpleExplanation: "A lessee can generally sub-lease an asset to someone else with the owner's permission. If they charge the sub-lessee the same or less than what they themselves pay, everyone agrees that's fine. But if they charge MORE and pocket the difference, scholars disagree - some (Hanafi) say that extra profit must go to charity, while others (Shafi'i, Hanbali - and the view the book prefers) say the sub-lessor can keep it. On costs: anything related to owning the asset (like major repairs, taxes) is the landlord/bank's job; day-to-day running costs (like electricity, routine upkeep) are the tenant's job. The bank can ask for a security deposit to protect itself, but if the tenant defaults, it can only recover its ACTUAL loss - never a bonus on top.",
      academicExplanation: "Sub-lease is permissible in principle, subject to the lessor's consent (which can be provided for in the lease agreement). All recognized schools agree sub-lease is valid if the sub-lease rent equals or is less than what the sub-lessor pays the original lessor. Views diverge where sub-lease rent EXCEEDS the original rent: Shafi'is and (as the preferred Hanbali view) allow the sub-lessor to keep the surplus; Imam Abu Hanifa holds the surplus must go to charity - UNLESS the sub-lessor has developed/improved the property or rents it in a different currency than what he pays the original lessor, in which case he may keep the surplus. Contemporary scholars prefer the Shafi'i/Hanbali view - sub-leasing is permissible regardless of rental amount. However, if a lessee sub-leases to MULTIPLE sub-lessees or invites others to merely SHARE rental income (without transferring any ownership portion) for a fee, this is NOT acceptable - it amounts to trading a receivable (the right to receive rent) at a discount, which is Riba. On security: since Ijarah creates a debt (payable rental), the lessor can demand security/guarantee from the lessee; taking security is also justified because the leased asset is held by the lessee as a trustee (fiduciary), who can be held liable for damage proven to result from his negligence. In cases of such loss or rental payment default, the lessor can recover the ACTUAL loss (excluding cost-of-funds/opportunity cost) from the security - any amount recovered as income beyond this would be Riba. On liabilities: a lessee/employee bears NO liability except where proven transgression, wilful waste, or damage - in which case he must compensate the lessor or replace the property. All ownership-derived liabilities fall on the lessor; usage-related liabilities fall on the lessee (e.g. for a leased house: property taxes = owner's responsibility; water/electricity/day-to-day usage expenses = lessee's responsibility). Contemporary Shari'ah experts link the lessor's expense obligation to whatever is customarily understood as necessary for the asset's continued performance/usufruct, with the parties free to agree otherwise. The leased property remains at the LESSOR's risk throughout the lease for factors beyond the lessee's control; the lessee is only liable to compensate for harm from his own misuse/negligence. A jointly-owned property can be leased out, with risk and rental distributed among co-owners per their ownership proportion.",
      examExplanation: "Sub-lease: permissible with lessor's consent. Sub-lease rent <= original rent: universally allowed. Sub-lease rent > original rent (surplus kept by sub-lessor): Shafi'i/Hanbali (preferred contemporary view) = allowed to keep surplus; Hanafi (Abu Hanifa) = surplus must go to charity UNLESS the sub-lessor improved the property or rents in a different currency. Sharing rental income across MULTIPLE sub-lessees without transferring ownership = NOT allowed (amounts to selling a receivable at a discount = Riba). Security: lessor can demand security/guarantee (Ijarah creates rental debt); recovery limited to ACTUAL loss (excluding opportunity cost/cost-of-funds) - any excess = Riba. Liability split: OWNERSHIP-related (taxes, major repairs) = LESSOR; USAGE-related (utilities, day-to-day upkeep) = LESSEE. Lessee liable ONLY for proven negligence/misuse/wilful damage; otherwise, asset stays at lessor's risk throughout.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Sub-leasing is permissible with the lessor's consent; all schools agree it's valid when sub-lease rent equals or is less than the original rent.",
        "When sub-lease rent exceeds the original rent, Shafi'i and (preferred) Hanbali views allow the sub-lessor to keep the surplus; the Hanafi view requires the surplus go to charity, UNLESS the sub-lessor improved the property or charges in a different currency than his own rent payment.",
        "Sharing rental income across multiple sub-lessees WITHOUT transferring any ownership portion is not permissible - this amounts to selling the right to receive rent (a receivable) at a discount, which is Riba.",
        "Ownership-related expenses/liabilities (e.g. property taxes, major repairs) fall on the LESSOR; usage-related expenses (e.g. utilities, day-to-day operation) fall on the LESSEE.",
        "The lessee bears NO liability for asset loss/damage except where proven transgression, wilful waste, or negligence on his part - otherwise, the asset remains at the lessor's risk throughout the lease.",
        "The lessor can demand security from the lessee, but on default/damage can recover only the ACTUAL loss (excluding cost-of-funds/opportunity cost) - any amount recovered beyond actual loss is Riba."
      ],
