      id: "ch11-t11",
      sectionNumber: "11.4.6",
      sectionTitle: "Takaful/Insurance Expenses",
      title: "Takaful and Insurance Expenses in Ijarah",
      overview: "Explains why Takaful is preferred over conventional insurance for leased assets, who should bear the cost as a matter of principle, and how banks recover it through rental while remaining accountable for any shortfall.",
      simpleExplanation: "Banks should ideally insure leased assets through Takaful (the Islamic alternative), not conventional insurance, though many still use conventional insurance temporarily due to limited Takaful availability. Since the bank owns the asset, it's technically the one with an 'insurable interest' and should be the policyholder - but banks are allowed to fold this cost into the rental they charge, as long as the customer agrees to that rental. If something goes wrong and full ownership transfer becomes impossible through no fault of the customer, the bank must refund him the difference between what he paid and the fair market rental - and if the Takaful payout doesn't cover the full loss, the bank (not the customer) absorbs the shortfall.",
      academicExplanation: "Many countries regulatorily require leasing entities to insure leased assets. Islamic banks should use Shari'ah-approved Takaful rather than conventional insurance (which involves Riba and Gharar), though many currently use conventional insurance due to insufficient Takaful availability - permitted by Shari'ah scholars only as a TRANSITIONAL measure. Scholars criticize the practice of Islamic banks passing Takaful premium costs onto the lessee as part of lease instalments, on the basis that the LESSOR has the insurable interest (as owner) and should bear the premium. This is technically correct - as asset owner, the bank should be the insurer/beneficiary of the Takaful policy. However, Islamic banks normally include Takaful expenses within the asset's acquisition cost when determining rental, which Shari'ah scholars permit on the basis that rental is subject to mutual consent - if the lessee agrees to the resulting rental amount, the contract remains Shari'ah-acceptable. Regarding insurable interest: it rightly belongs to the bank as lessor. But if transfer of ownership becomes impossible for reasons NOT attributable to the lessee, the lessee must be protected by being paid the difference between the rent he actually paid (per the lease agreement) and the fair market rental of similar assets - some Islamic banks fail to observe this rule, which the author flags as against the spirit of Islamic finance, calling for Shari'ah advisor attention. On loss/damage effects: it is usually stipulated that the lessee receives a copy of the Takaful policy and must observe its conditions; if the asset is destroyed and the LESSEE's failure to observe policy conditions bars recovery, the lessee is held liable. Absent any fault/negligence by the lessee, the LESSOR bears all responsibility for asset damage/loss. If the Takaful claim paid is LESS than the actual loss/expense incurred, the shortfall CANNOT be charged to the lessee - the bank itself must bear it.",
      examExplanation: "Takaful preferred over conventional insurance (Riba/Gharar concerns); conventional insurance permitted only transitionally where Takaful is unavailable. Insurable interest belongs to the BANK (as owner) - it should technically be the policyholder/beneficiary. BUT banks may include Takaful cost within acquisition cost when calculating rental (permitted via mutual consent principle) - just can't pass it as a separate, unexplained charge. Key protection: if ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons NOT attributable to the lessee, the bank must refund him the difference between rent paid and fair market rental. Loss allocation: lessee liable only if HIS failure to follow Takaful policy conditions causes non-recovery; otherwise, lessor bears all loss/damage responsibility, including any shortfall between Takaful claim paid and actual loss (never passed to the lessee).",
      keyPoints: [
        "Islamic banks should use Takaful (not conventional insurance, due to Riba/Gharar) for leased assets; conventional insurance is permitted only as a transitional measure where Takaful is unavailable.",
        "The insurable interest for a leased asset rightly belongs to the LESSOR (bank), as the owner - it should technically be the Takaful policyholder and beneficiary.",
        "Banks may include Takaful expenses within the asset's acquisition cost when calculating rental (permitted via the principle of mutual consent to the resulting rental), rather than charging it as a separate itemized cost to the lessee.",
        "If ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons NOT attributable to the lessee, the bank must refund him the difference between the rent he actually paid and the fair market rental of similar assets - a rule the author notes some banks fail to observe.",
        "If a Takaful claim payout is LESS than the actual loss/expense incurred, the shortfall cannot be charged to the lessee - the bank must bear it, unless the lessee's own failure to follow policy conditions caused the shortfall."
      ],
