  chapterSummary: "Chapter 11 establishes Ijarah as the sale of usufruct (not corpus) - a contract where ownership and its associated risk always remain with the lessor, distinguishing it fundamentally from Bai' (sale). The chapter works through the general juristic rules (valid subject matter, rent determination, sub-leasing, liability allocation, termination), then critiques conventional financial and security leases for shifting rent-accrual timing and ownership risk onto the lessee in ways that conflict with Shari'ah. It presents Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek as the solution - a genuine lease kept structurally separate from a unilateral, lessor-binding promise to transfer ownership at lease end - and walks through the full bank procedure, operational pitfalls, and risk-management toolkit. The chapter closes by showing how leased assets (though not bare rental rights) can be securitized into Ijarah Sukuk, giving Ijarah substantial - and still largely unrealized - potential across retail, corporate, and public-sector financing.",
